Bayesian Modelling Using R-INLA (BMIN04)<https://prstats.org/course/bayesian-modelling-using-r-inla-bmin04/?utm_source=chatgpt.com>

Delivered by Dr. Virgilio G?mez-Rubio, a statistician with deep expertise in Bayesian inference, spatial statistics, statistical computing, and the application of Integrated Nested Laplace Approximation (INLA) to complex data. He is the author of Bayesian Inference with INLA and has extensive experience developing and applying Bayesian and spatial modelling methods in R.

Learn how to use Bayesian modelling and R-INLA to analyse hierarchical, spatial, temporal, and other complex ecological datasets.

Ecological data frequently contain hierarchical structure, spatial and temporal dependence, repeated observations, missing values, and multiple sources of uncertainty. Bayesian modelling provides a powerful framework for handling these complexities, while INLA offers an efficient approach for fitting many sophisticated Bayesian models without the computational demands of conventional MCMC methods. This course provides practical, hands-on training in Bayesian inference and R-INLA, with methods directly applicable to ecological and environmental research.

What you'll gain

  *   A strong understanding of Bayesian inference and prior specification
  *   Practical experience fitting Bayesian models using R-INLA
  *   Skills fitting GLMMs and multilevel models
  *   Experience analysing hierarchical ecological datasets
  *   Practical training in spatial Bayesian modelling
  *   Skills for analysing time-series and spatiotemporal data
  *   Understanding of latent effects and advanced INLA model structures
  *   Experience comparing models using DIC and WAIC
  *   Skills interpreting posterior distributions and quantifying uncertainty
  *   Approaches for handling missing values and imputation
  *   Confidence applying Bayesian modelling workflows to your own ecological datasets

Course format

  *   5-day live, instructor-led online course
  *   7 hours of training per day
  *   Combination of lectures and hands-on practical exercises
  *   Dedicated practical sessions throughout
  *   Opportunity to discuss and work with participants' own datasets
  *   All code, datasets, and presentation materials provided
  *   All sessions recorded
  *   30 days of recording access and post-course email support

Who is this course for?

  *   Ecologists and quantitative ecologists
  *   Wildlife and conservation researchers
  *   Spatial ecologists
  *   Population and community ecologists
  *   Environmental scientists
  *   Researchers analysing monitoring and long-term ecological datasets
  *   Researchers working with hierarchical, spatial, or temporal data
  *   PhD students and researchers wanting to develop Bayesian modelling skills in R

Participants should have basic familiarity with R and regression modelling.

Why take this course?

Ecological datasets rarely consist of simple independent observations. Researchers commonly encounter sites nested within regions, repeated measurements through time, spatial autocorrelation, environmental variation, missing observations, and multiple levels of biological variation.

R-INLA is particularly useful for these kinds of problems because it provides an efficient framework for fitting sophisticated hierarchical, spatial, and spatiotemporal Bayesian models. These approaches have applications across species distributions, population dynamics, abundance and occurrence data, ecological monitoring, environmental relationships, and spatial ecology.

The course progresses from the foundations of Bayesian inference and INLA through GLMMs, multilevel models, time series, priors, spatial models, latent effects, and missing-data problems, before concluding with case studies and participants' own data.

Whether you're analysing species distributions, population trends, environmental monitoring data, spatial ecological patterns, repeated surveys, or other complex ecological datasets, this course will give you a practical framework for incorporating Bayesian inference into your ecological research.

