Population Genomics and Structure Analysis (PGSA01) ? Applications for Ecology

Population Genomics and Structure Analysis (PGSA01)<https://prstats.org/course/population-genomics-and-structure-analysis-pgsa01/?utm_source=chatgpt.com>

Delivered by Dr. Nikolay Oskolkov, a bioinformatician, computational biologist, and data scientist with extensive experience in population genomics, evolutionary biology, environmental DNA, ancient DNA, statistical modelling, dimensionality reduction, clustering, and computational biology.

Learn how to analyse population structure, genetic differentiation, ancestry, gene flow, introgression, and genomic variation, with methods highly applicable to molecular ecology, conservation genetics, landscape genetics, and evolutionary ecology.

Understanding how genetic variation is distributed within and among populations is fundamental to ecology. Population genomic approaches can reveal population connectivity, genetic differentiation, demographic history, admixture, introgression, and signals of selection, helping researchers investigate how populations respond to environmental variation, geographic isolation, habitat fragmentation, and other ecological processes. This course provides practical training in the statistical and computational methods used to analyse population structure using modern genomic datasets.

What you'll gain

  *   Practical skills for processing and analysing population genomic data
  *   Understanding of genetic variation, SNP discovery, and variant calling
  *   Experience analysing low-coverage sequencing data using ANGSD
  *   Skills using PCA, MDS, t-SNE, and UMAP to explore population structure
  *   Understanding of the effects of sampling design on population genomic inference
  *   Practical experience with ADMIXTURE, STRUCTURE, and NGSadmix
  *   Skills investigating ancestry and admixture among populations
  *   Experience using ChromoPainter and fineSTRUCTURE to investigate fine-scale population structure
  *   Understanding and interpretation of FST, F2, and F3 statistics
  *   Approaches for conducting selection scans and identifying population differentiation
  *   Experience using F4 and D-statistics to investigate introgression and gene flow
  *   Confidence interpreting genomic patterns in an ecological and evolutionary context

Course format

  *   5-day live, instructor-led online course
  *   25 hours of training
  *   Practical analysis of population genomic datasets
  *   Hands-on experience with widely used population-genetic software
  *   Training across R, Python, and computational genomic workflows
  *   All course materials provided
  *   All sessions recorded
  *   30 days of recording access and post-course email support

Who is this course for?

  *   Molecular ecologists
  *   Evolutionary ecologists
  *   Conservation geneticists and genomic researchers
  *   Population geneticists
  *   Landscape and spatial geneticists
  *   Researchers studying population connectivity and differentiation
  *   Researchers investigating adaptation, gene flow, and introgression
  *   Ecologists incorporating genomic data into their research
  *   Postgraduate students and early-career researchers

A basic background in genetics and molecular biology is expected, together with some familiarity with R, Python, or Bash. Previous experience with population genetics or next-generation sequencing is beneficial but not essential.

Why take this course?

Ecological processes leave signatures in the genomes of populations. Dispersal, geographic isolation, habitat fragmentation, environmental gradients, population declines, colonisation, and interactions between populationscan all influence patterns of genomic variation.

Population genomics provides powerful tools for identifying these patterns. Methods such as PCA and ancestry clustering can reveal genetic structure, while F-statistics can quantify population differentiation and help reconstruct population history. Selection scans can identify unusually differentiated genomic regions, and D-statistics provide tools for investigating gene flow and introgression.

These approaches have particularly strong applications in molecular ecology, conservation genetics, evolutionary ecology, landscape genetics, population connectivity, local adaptation, and the study of demographic history.

Whether you're investigating population structure, connectivity, genetic differentiation, adaptation, admixture, introgression, or the genomic consequences of ecological and environmental change, this course provides the practical tools needed to incorporate population genomic analyses into ecological research.

