Why take this course?

Species distribution models have become fundamental tools for understanding how species relate to their environments. They are used to investigate habitat suitability, environmental preferences, geographic distributions and potential responses to environmental change, as well as to support biodiversity monitoring and conservation planning.

Traditional SDM approaches can struggle with nonlinear relationships, interactions among predictors and uncertainty. Bayesian Additive Regression Trees provide a flexible, non-parametric Bayesian alternative, capable of modelling complex species?environment relationships without requiring researchers to specify their exact functional form in advance.

A major advantage of the Bayesian framework is its ability to explicitly represent uncertainty in model predictions. The course therefore goes beyond simply producing distribution maps, showing participants how to evaluate models, examine credible intervals, assess variable importance and use partial dependence to understand the ecological relationships driving predictions.

Whether you're working with species distributions, habitat suitability, biodiversity, conservation, biogeography, climate?species relationships or ecological niche modelling, this course provides a practical introduction to applying modern Bayesian methods to species distribution data.

