Delivered by Dr Ignacio Morales-Castilla, a biogeographer and macroecologist whose research integrates ecological, evolutionary and biogeographical approaches to understand biodiversity patterns, community structure, niche evolution and species distributions. His work includes integrating phylogenetic, functional and geographic information into ecological models.

Learn how to integrate phylogenetic relationships, spatial data and Bayesian mixed modelling in R to investigate ecological patterns while accounting for the shared evolutionary history of species.

Ecological datasets frequently contain observations from multiple species that cannot be treated as statistically independent because closely related species tend to share traits and ecological characteristics. This course introduces spatial phylogenetics and Bayesian Phylogenetic Mixed Models (PMMs), providing practical methods for incorporating phylogenetic covariance into hierarchical ecological models.

What you'll gain

