The course is particularly suited to researchers interested in biodiversity patterns, macroecology, community ecology and conservation, as well as those working with species trait, comparative and spatial biodiversity datasets.

Why take this course?

Ecologists routinely compare traits, distributions and environmental responses across multiple species. However, species are linked by their shared evolutionary history, meaning observations from different species may not be statistically independent. Ignoring this structure can affect ecological inference.

Bayesian Phylogenetic Mixed Models provide a way to incorporate this evolutionary relatedness directly into a familiar hierarchical modelling framework. Participants learn how to specify phylogenetic covariance structures, estimate phylogenetic effects and determine when including phylogeny improves an ecological model.

The spatial component extends these methods to questions about how evolutionary diversity is distributed geographically. Participants will learn to investigate and visualise phylogenetic diversity, community structure, phylogenetic turnover and spatial biodiversity patterns, before integrating phylogenetic information into Bayesian models.

These approaches have applications across community ecology, macroecology, conservation, biodiversity science, species trait analysis and biogeography, particularly where researchers want to understand ecological patterns across multiple related species while correctly accounting for their evolutionary relationships.

